{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Snowmelt Lens: Nitrogen Fixers Trapped Under Baffin Bay Ice",
    "date": "2026-11-20",
    "lesson": "In spring, meltwater pools on top of Arctic sea ice and seeps down to form a buoyant, fresher 'lens' trapped just under the ice, isolated from the saltier water below by a sharp density step. This lens is nitrogen-starved - almost no nitrate reaches it from below - so the cyanobacteria living there survive mainly by fixing their own nitrogen from dissolved N2, a costly, oxygen-sensitive process. Their payoff is light: the lens sits right against the ice, in the brightest water of the whole column, while just a few meters down nutrient-richer water is almost dark under the remaining snow cover. The scenario asks whether a diazotroph founder can bank enough energy during the long polar day to survive the night, using carbohydrate exudate as its main food while the halocline physically pens it against the ice.",
    "citation": "Arctic melt-pond and under-ice meltwater lens microbial studies (e.g. Fernandez-Mendez et al., under-ice algal/bacterial communities)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Baffin Bay land-fast ice spring melt, under-ice meltwater lens microbiology"
  },
